WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.080
Have you ever seen a hand
knitting pattern that you love?

2
00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:03.580
and

3
00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:07.100
thought, "I can knit that on the machine"?

4
00:00:07.130 --> 00:00:10.090
Chances are you can. Let's take a look

5
00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:14.520
at evaluating a hand knitting pattern
and walk through some of the steps

6
00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:19.520
for converting it to machine knitting.
We'll ask three questions.

7
00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:22.060
Can it be done?

8
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.340
Examine the basic shape of the sweater

9
00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:28.820
and the pieces and shaping
that needs to be done.

10
00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:32.700
Is this appropriate for the machine?

11
00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:35.900
If there are large, drapey pieces,

12
00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:39.960
or a very wide pieces,
they could be done either with "U"

13
00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:44.900
knitting with a ribber or
put in some seams.

14
00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:48.620
Second question, is it practical?

15
00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:52.660
Examine the yarn and the stitch patterns.

16
00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.780
If the stitch patterns are mostly Garter

17
00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:57.780
stitch, it probably won't work unless you want

18
00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:01.000
to spend a whole lot
of time reforming stitches.

19
00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.180
Maybe the yarn has sequins in it,

20
00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:08.740
or has an unusual construction
that might not work on the machine.

21
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:12.280
something to take into consideration.

22
00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:18.340
Plain stockinette garments
make a whole lot of sense.

23
00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:21.130
A other techniques like entrelac

24
00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:24.740
well, it can be done, but do you
want a spend that much time?

25
00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:28.160
A heavy cables with popcorn stitch

26
00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:32.540
and a lot of Purl stitch accents,  again,
very time-consuming.

27
00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:34.520
Is it practical?

28
00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:39.780
If it's a lace sweater,
how are your programming skills?

29
00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:44.020
Are you willing to hand manipulate
lace over a whole sweater?

30
00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:46.720
Which leads us to the third question.

31
00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:52.210
Are you willing to do what it takes
to knit this garment on the machine?

32
00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:57.060
When I hand knit, it's for the process
of knitting a feeling the yarn,

33
00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:01.360
the joy of watching complicated
stitch patterns develop.

34
00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:02.620
And it's relaxing.

35
00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:04.850
Plus, it's portable.

36
00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:07.100
Examine the hand knitting pattern.

37
00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:12.320
Are you willing to sit a the machine doing
a lot of hand manipulation and hand

38
00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:17.500
shaping, maybe transferring a whole
bunch of stitches or reforming stitches?

39
00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:22.170
If the pattern you're looking
at a met all of these criteria.

40
00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:23.660
Where do you start?

41
00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:27.890
The critical part of this
process is matching the gauge.

42
00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:30.320
Unless you want to do a lot of math

43
00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:35.400
and re-chart the pattern
or enter it into some knitting software.

44
00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:38.500
You must match the pattern gauge.
Exactly.

45
00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:41.420
Not close, but exactly.

46
00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:46.120
Now double check that
there are enough needles on your needle bed for the size.

47
00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:52.380
you want to knit. Find the largest
cast on edge, usually the sweater back.

48
00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.200
Multiply it by the stitch gauge.

49
00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:02.380
Occasionally the bind off edge or a band
or a collar may have too many stitches.

50
00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:02.520


51
00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:08.610
Determine all that before you start
knitting, you may be able to plan a seam

52
00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:14.020
in a band in an inconspicuous place,
or a you might consider hand knitting

53
00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:16.940
a section that's too large
for the knitting machine.

54
00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:19.680
You'll have to do a little bit of math.

55
00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:21.980
Because a machine knitters can't

56
00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:25.580
accurately measure the length of their
knitting as they knit,

57
00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:31.240
they need to convert the length
indicated in the pattern to rows.

58
00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:35.580
If there's a schematic,
it makes your job easier.

59
00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:38.180
Just highlight the dimensions
for your size.

60
00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:40.060
And do the math.

61
00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:43.000
This sample sweater has 3 inches

62
00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:48.100
of ribbing. I've multiplied
3 times the row gauge.

63
00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:48.820
A.

64
00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:52.820
I'll know that I need
to knit 10 rows of ribbing.

65
00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:53.940


66
00:03:53.970 --> 00:04:00.900
The body of the sweater is
15.5 inches long or 50 rows.

67
00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:06.500
The Raglan shaping is worked
over 9 inches or 30 rows.

68
00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:12.120
Repeat this for all the piece of your
pattern before you start knitting.

69
00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.980
If there's no schematic with the pattern,

70
00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:20.120
read through the knitting
instructions. Again,

71
00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:23.500
look for that largest Cast on or bind off

72
00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:25.940
to determine if it will
fit on your needle bed.

73
00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:27.080


74
00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:32.320
Then look for any length measurements.

75
00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:39.600
The text would read "knit
until the piece measures so many inches".

76
00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:44.080
Or "knit in pattern for so many inches".

77
00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:47.960
Multiply the dimensions given by the row

78
00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:55.280
gauge and substitute your row gauge when
you're knitting. If there's no schematic,

79
00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:58.020
a always find it easier
to sketch out my own.

80
00:04:58.040 --> 00:04:59.820
A insert the numbers,

81
00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:05.180
noting both the row counts and the stitch
counts at the various shaping points.

82
00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:08.920
This really helps when knitting.

83
00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:15.740
Here's a hand knit schematic for a sweater
that's knit in all in one piece.

84
00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:22.520
By adding side seams, we'll have enough
needles to knit this on the machine.

85
00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:26.280
Keep in mind that since we're adding

86
00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:29.980
side seam, we'll need
to add seaming stitches.

87
00:05:30.010 --> 00:05:33.840
Here are the cast on numbers.

88
00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:37.580
We'll knit the length
a instructed by the pattern,

89
00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:38.800
up to the armhole

90
00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:43.180
bind offs. The hand knit pattern reads "knit

91
00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:48.240
45; bind off 10; knit 90;
bind off 10; knit 45.

92
00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:49.140


93
00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:52.320
Then the fronts and backs
are worked separately.

94
00:05:54.280 --> 00:06:00.460
We'll be knitting the body and the armhole
and neckline shaping in pieces,

95
00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:03.500
and we need to remember to add
those seaming stitches.

96
00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:07.160
So here are our machine
knitting instructions.

97
00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:12.500
The remaining armhole a neckline shaping
would be exactly according to pattern.

98
00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:16.540
Now you can knit this sweater
in a fraction of the time.

99
00:06:16.570 --> 00:06:22.700
I encourage you to take a few minutes
to surf the web or look a some sweaters in

100
00:06:22.730 --> 00:06:27.900
catalogs or stores which sweaters
could be made on the machine?

101
00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:31.020
Which ones would be a better knit by hand?

102
00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:32.800
Train your eye and

103
00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:38.720
open yourself to a whole new world
of knitting patterns for your machine.

